From d5625eae25c6eaf296458ab60ed605ebe84b0cb1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 18:24:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 044/206] feat(shorebird): the real Sim->CPU wrapper body --
 first stub body with real bytes

Task 6b-iii, which needed 6b-i (Thread::is_simulating_) and 6b-ii (the brk
hypercall block) first. Of their 15 private stub generators this is the first one
in this fork with a body rather than a Breakpoint placeholder.

Seven instructions, transcribed one at a time from their
GenerateTransitionDartToCpuIfNeededStub at 0x10046c5f0 (WRAPPER_STUBS.md
section 7). It is not the masked thunk this plan originally assumed -- it masks
nothing, and branches on Thread::is_simulating_.

**Six of their seven words come out byte-identical.** That is the real result,
and it needed its own test: the size assertion is blind to register and opcode
choice, which is exactly how the pad register (R9 vs their IP0) and the stub
names slipped through earlier in this work. The new test reads the emitted body
out of the stub and compares words. The fourth word encodes is_simulating_'s
offset -- 0x8d8 for them, larger here because our field is appended -- so it is
checked by re-deriving the encoding, and the test also asserts that the same
expression reproduces their 0xf9446f51 from 0x8d8.

Three negative controls, run and each red:

  - perturbing the expected brk word by 1 -> "expected: <3560318369> but was:
    <3560318368>", so the test reads the real bytes rather than restating
    constants.
  - an extra nop in the body -> the pad-array test fails with 16380 vs 16384.
    This one matters: ASSERT_EQUAL in the generator is compiled out in release
    and product, so it never runs in any build we make. The unit test is what
    holds the body to 28 bytes, not that assertion.
  - the earlier 6b-ii controls still apply to the brk arm this body emits.

target::Thread cannot carry is_simulating_ (see thread.h), so the generator uses
the host offset. That is only the target offset while the layouts agree, which is
now checked rather than assumed: a RELEASE_ASSERT compares the extracted offset
for a neighbouring Thread field against its host value, so a cross-architecture
gen_snapshot fails loudly instead of emitting a wrong displacement.

CPU->Sim keeps its Breakpoint placeholder -- 272 bytes calling
compiler::RunOnSimulator, which is Phase D -- and CPUToSimConfig::GetStubCodeRange
still reports an empty range, so no page is mapped from it and no pad of it can
be entered.

Unchanged and honestly reported: wrapper_symbols_check.sh still prints 0 and
exits 1. Emitting a body creates no caller of WrapperAllocator; that needs code
on the transition path actually wrapping an entry point. Nothing here has been
run on a device, and the "continue from LR" reading of their handler is still
inference.

76/76 shorebird, sim_bridge, sim_transition and shorebird_sim_to_cpu tests pass
by exit code and by absence of "error:". Product gen_snapshot links.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

